{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-84840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/0d278fe1bf34cd60877be6886c90e1734fab7433f9f94ad7765d379e69c2da42.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(2,2),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,6),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "baa116a0cf27ebc7f340f7c99720971ce5b2e739038cef8ee9c2bb07924aa4d8"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+2=2{t}^{2},\\,y+2=4t"] ["math" "x+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=t"] ["math" "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2t"] ["math" "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t"] ["math" "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-84840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/assets/ba3d2c8719c97911d2339548f08601b07bcfe555d59dbb058a157836c45a34fe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-4,2),$ and passes through the point $(-5,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-4,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-1=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+4=(-1){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(-1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "baa116a0cf27ebc7f340f7c99720971ce5b2e739038cef8ee9c2bb07924aa4d8"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+4=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t"] ["math" "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t"] ["math" "x+4={t}^{2},\\,y-2=t"] ["math" "x-4=-{t}^{2},\\,y+2=-2t"] ["math" "x-4={t}^{2},\\,y+2=2t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-84843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+4=-{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-2t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-106500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=4t,\\,y+5=2{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-106500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+3}{4},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{y+5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{y+5}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{y+5}{2} \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =8(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-106500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "baa116a0cf27ebc7f340f7c99720971ce5b2e739038cef8ee9c2bb07924aa4d8"} {"question" "q-106500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-106500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+3)^{2}=4(y+5)"] ["math" "(x+3)^{2}=8(y+5)"] ["math" "(x+5)^{2}=8(y+3)"] ["math" "(x-3)^{2}=8(y+5)"] ["math" "(x-3)^{2}=8(y-5)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-106500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+3)^{2}=8(y+5)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=4{t}^{2},\\,y-1=8t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+2}{4},\\,t=\\frac{y-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-1}{8})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+2}{4}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{64} & =\\frac{x+2}{4} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =64\\cdot (\\frac{x+2}{4}) \\\\ {(y-1)}^{2} & =16(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323221" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "baa116a0cf27ebc7f340f7c99720971ce5b2e739038cef8ee9c2bb07924aa4d8"} {"question" "q-323221" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323221" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323221" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323221" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16(x+2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-2=-t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-4(x+1),\\,t=-(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-(y-2))}^{2} \\\\  & =(y-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4(x+1)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =-4(x+1) \\\\ {(y-2)}^{2} & =-4(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323230" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "baa116a0cf27ebc7f340f7c99720971ce5b2e739038cef8ee9c2bb07924aa4d8"} {"question" "q-323230" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323230" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323230" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"} {"question" "q-323230" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4(x+1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039044/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa85867f5a282f1b02d24a9e056914fe25d5d27a19caa81f17ef24e45f8bd12c"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1338 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
